const GENERATION = "## Where candidates come from (when asked to generate)\n\nJace, 2026-11-23: \"I think a lot of the big questions spawned from patterns being recognized and then asking if it applies to other areas or forcing the pattern to continue to it's natural conclusion. What are some general processes that lead to other deep questions to be asked? Can we incorporate those into our instructions for asking the questions?\" The research behind this section, with its sources, is in ledger/question-generation-research-2026-11-23.md. Its finding in one sentence: a deep question is almost never invented from nothing; it is derived from one specific thing already known, by one of a small number of repeatable moves. So a draft is a derivation, not an inspiration, and this section says how to make one. It changes how drafts are produced and nothing else: every gate, the Depth requirement and the Ambition requirement apply to the result exactly as before.\n\n### How to draft, whatever the method\n\n1. **Seeds before questions.** Before writing any question, collect seeds. A seed is one specific, established thing about the world in the batch's area: a measured regularity, a theorem, an accepted mechanism, a reproducible anomaly, a classification, a stated impossibility, a dated event, an attested text or custom, a rule of law. A specialist must accept the seed without argument; name its source unless it is textbook material. \"Recent progress in X\" and \"the literature on X\" are topics, not seeds.\n2. **One move per draft.** Apply one method below to one seed, following that method's steps in order. The seed is the conventional part and the move supplies the single unconventional step. A draft whose seed is itself speculative is a guess stacked on a guess, and a draft with no unconventional step is routine work; discard both.\n3. **Deepen once.** Put to the resulting question: \"why would the answer be what it is?\" and \"what would have to be true of the world for this to be so?\" If either gives a well-formed question that is larger, draft that one instead. Do this once, not repeatedly.\n4. **Record the derivation.** Every draft carries a derivation line in three parts: \"Seed: the specific thing, with its source. Move: the method and the step taken. Why the question follows: one sentence.\" A draft with no nameable seed is not submitted.\n5. **Spread.** Every method is used at least once and none more than twice. No two drafts share a seed. Fifteen drafts that are fifteen variations on one idea are one draft.\n6. **The first question that comes to mind has been asked.** A famous question, or the obvious application of a move to a famous seed, is prior art. The gain is in applying a move to a seed that has not had it applied, so prefer seeds that are solid but unglamorous: a regularity noted in passing, a hypothesis nobody examines, a cell of a classification nobody mentions.\n\nThe historical instances below show what each move looks like when it worked. Every one was asked long ago. They are demonstrations of a move, never candidates and never templates to reword.\n\n### The methods\n\n- **Anomaly mining.** Take an observation that is reported, reproducible, and either at odds with what the accepted account predicts or outside anything the account speaks to. Write the sentence \"X is observed; if H were true, X would be a matter of course\" and ask H as a question about the world. Prefer anomalies mentioned in passing and never made anyone's subject. Instances: the excess advance of Mercury's perihelion; the shape of the black-body spectrum.\n- **Presupposition inversion.** Take a settled claim about a mechanism, or a theorem. List everything it needs in order to be true: each hypothesis, each idealisation, each restriction of regime. Choose one that has never been tested, or one that is assumed but may not be needed, and ask what happens when it is dropped, weakened or negated. In mathematics: remove one hypothesis and ask whether the conclusion survives, or ask for the weakest hypothesis that still gives it. Instances: dropping the parallel postulate; dropping absolute simultaneity.\n- **Cross-field transplant (does the pattern apply elsewhere?).** Take a pattern, law or structure established in a field A outside the batch's area (in the open turn: outside the draft's own field), and an object in the batch's area B that resembles A's object. Write the mapping: which things correspond and which relations between them correspond. Sort the relations into three lists: known to hold in B, known to fail in B, never checked in B. The question comes from the third list only. Map relations, never surface resemblance, and prefer a source field far from the target, since near transplants have usually been made. State the question in B's own vocabulary. Instances: Weil's dictionary between number fields and function fields; Schrödinger asking a physicist's question about heredity; spin-glass physics carried to associative memory.\n- **New-instrument questions.** Take something recently made measurable. Name what the instrument resolves that nothing before it did (a scale, a timescale, a number of objects at once) and ask about a phenomenon that only exists at that resolution, not about the instrument.\n- **Silence mapping.** Take a phenomenon with a dense literature of how-answers. Write out the kinds of question that can be asked of it: how it works, how it comes into being, what it does for the system it is part of, how it came to be this way, and why this way and not another. Find the kind with no literature and ask it.\n- **Unification and coincidence.** Either take two unexplained anomalies, possibly in different fields, and ask whether one cause could produce both; or take two quantities, exponents or structures that agree with no known reason, and ask whether the agreement is a law and what would make it one. Instances: the equality of inertial and gravitational mass; 196884 = 196883 + 1.\n- **Limits.** Take a quantity, a process or a capability that has varied or improved across many cases. Ask whether a bound exists, what sets it, and whether it can be attained. Instances: Carnot's bound on heat engines; Shannon's on channels; Turing's on computation.\n- **Capability inversion.** Name a capability or technology now treated as out of reach, write down what would have to be true of the world for it to work, and ask whether anyone has asked that.\n- **Textbook inversion.** Take a claim every introductory text in the field states as settled: what does it presuppose that has never been tested?\n- **Pattern continuation (force the pattern to its natural conclusion).** Take a regularity that holds across a range: a scaling law, a run of cases n = 1, 2, 3, a trend across sizes, energies, dimensions or levels of generality. Continue it past where anyone has looked: to the next case, to the extreme of the parameter (zero, infinity, the smallest, the largest, the fastest), or to full generality. Then ask whichever of three questions is live: does the pattern hold there; if it must break, where does it break and what replaces it; if it holds, what does it force to exist. Take seriously the solutions of an equation or the outputs of a model that are usually discarded as unphysical. Instances: Dirac keeping his equation's negative-energy solutions; Einstein asking what a light wave looks like to someone moving alongside it; Chandrasekhar following the white-dwarf mass-radius relation to zero radius.\n- **Empty cell.** Take a classification of the area's objects by two or more independent properties and lay out every combination. Find a combination with no known occupant, or something the accepted theory permits and nobody has observed. Ask whether the occupant exists, and if it does not, what forbids it. An absence the theory does not explain is an anomaly in its own right. Instances: the gaps in Mendeleev's table; magnetic monopoles; Fermi's \"where is everybody?\".\n- **Symmetry and its breaking.** List the transformations that leave the seed unchanged (a law, a structure, a body plan, an algorithm), and the ones that might have and do not. Then ask one of three things. For a symmetry that holds: what does it conserve or forbid, and has that consequence been looked for. For a symmetry that fails: what broke it, and is the outcome forced or an accident that could have gone the other way. For a statement with a converse, a dual or an inverse direction (the cause recovered from the effect, the object from its invariants): has that direction been asked. Instances: Noether's link from symmetries to conservation laws; the single handedness of biological molecules; \"can one hear the shape of a drum?\".\n- **Theory collision.** Take two results, each accepted in its own domain. Find a regime, an object or a limit where both claim to apply, and work out what each says there. If they disagree, the question is what actually happens there. Instances: Maxwell's electrodynamics and Newton's mechanics applied to moving bodies; quantum mechanics and relativity applied to a dense star; general relativity and quantum field theory applied at a horizon.\n- **Unlinked literatures.** Find an intermediate B (a mechanism, a molecule, a structure, a mathematical object) that one literature connects to A and a separate literature connects to C, where the two literatures do not cite each other. Ask whether A bears on C through B. The question is about A and C in the world; the two literatures are how it was found and never what it is about. Instance: Swanson's 1986 link from fish oil through blood viscosity to Raynaud's syndrome.\n\nEach method is aimed at the world, not at the literature about it: anomaly mining mines observations of the world, not of reporting; presupposition inversion inverts a claim about a mechanism, not about a method; cross-field transplant transplants a question about an object, not a QA procedure; new-instrument questions ask what the instrument makes visible about its target, not whether its records are consistent; silence mapping maps why-questions about phenomena; an empty cell is a missing thing, not a missing paper; unlinked literatures yields a question about A and C, not about who cites whom. A draft that any method turns into an audit of papers, records or practice fails the Depth requirement as before."

